success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"grade\":\"wrong\",\"rationale\":\"The answer omits the central definition in Rule 6c-11(a)(1), under which an AP is a member or participant of an SEC-registered clearing agency with a written agreement with the ETF or one of its service providers allowing it to place creation/redemption orders. It also wrongly says the rule does not detail permitted AP counterparties, when the rule does specify the ETF or its service providers. It describes APs as 'broker-dealers acting under fund arrangements,' which is inaccurate. It covers none of the reviewed-form facts: the distributor and AP as main counterparties, the transfer agent countersigning, and the trust as a third-party beneficiary that sometimes signs for specified provisions. It mentions NSCC/DTC generally but does not state the forms' NSCC netting membership or DTC participation requirement, and it does not note that eligibility differs from being a market maker. Abstaining on the forms is reasonable, but the rule-level content it does give is materially wrong or missing.\"}","ttft_ms":3362,"type":"result","duration_ms":3378,"uuid":"d2801f86-4d2e-4885-8cac-1c94124fd851","ttft_stream_ms":576,"time_to_request_ms":72,"first_content_frame_ms":577,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
